\section{Combined Picture: Three-Tilt Force-Depth Curves}

\Cref{fig:three-tilts} integrates the depth and tilt sweeps for
\texttt{finray\_26}.

\begin{figure}[h]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.95\linewidth]{finray_26_Fz_vs_depth_three_tilts.png}
\caption[Three-tilt depth curves]{Stationary force vs depth for
\texttt{finray\_26} at $0^{\circ}$, $+2.5^{\circ}$ x, and $+5^{\circ}$ x
tilts. Background shading marks the regime boundaries identified in
experiment 1. The three tilt conditions diverge at boundary depths and
converge in the soft plateau.}
\label{fig:three-tilts}
\end{figure}

The three lines diverge in the boundary regime and re-converge in the soft
plateau. Notably, the convergence is complete by $+10$\,mm; \emph{the soft
plateau is tilt-robust both in magnitude and in sign}.

\section{Rotation Tracking Verification}

A natural concern is whether the Franka arm faithfully realises the
commanded tilt orientation. The 30\,Hz session log records actual and
commanded RPY at every sample, enabling a direct measurement of orientation
tracking error
\begin{equation}
  \delta(t) = \| \mathrm{RPY}_{\text{actual}}(t) -
                 \mathrm{RPY}_{\text{cmd}}(t) \|_2.
\end{equation}
Across five representative sessions, the mean steady-state $\delta$ was
$0.05^{\circ}$ with a worst-case $0.13^{\circ}$. Transient excursions up to
$\sim 5^{\circ}$ occur exclusively within the \texttt{orientation\_lock}
stage at session start, before any descent or grasp, and are filtered out
of trial-level analyses.

A $2.5^{\circ}$ commanded tilt is therefore $50\times$ the noise floor of
the arm's rotation tracking; the observed force differences cannot be
attributed to arm rotation error.

% =============================================================================
\chapter{Discussion}
\label{ch:discussion}

\section{Depth-Dependent Tilt Robustness}

The central empirical finding of this thesis is summarised by
\cref{fig:tilt-mag-sweep} and \cref{fig:y-tilt-asym}: the finray's
sensitivity to tilt is a strong function of press depth.
\begin{itemize}
  \item At boundary depths ($-0.5$ to $0$\,mm relative to the detected
        surface): a $\pm 5^{\circ}$ tilt about either axis produces $30$--$58\%$
        changes in contact force, with the y-axis (asymmetric) tilt also
        producing a $1.4\times$ to $2.4\times$ sign asymmetry.
  \item At the safe depth ($+10$\,mm): the same $\pm 5^{\circ}$ tilts produce
        less than $1\%$ change in contact force, and the sign asymmetry vanishes
        to within $1\%$.
\end{itemize}

We propose the following mechanistic explanation. At light contact, only the
distal end of the finray finger touches the cloth; the contact zone is
sensitive to the angle of approach, and the geometric asymmetry of the
finger ridge (visible in cross-section) translates into a direction-dependent
force response. At deep contact, the finger wraps along its entire length
around the cloth and table, presenting an effective contact surface that
integrates over the finger geometry; the asymmetry is averaged out by the
distributed contact mechanics.

\section{Implications for Cloth-Handling System Design}

The conventional wisdom that compliant grippers are universally more robust
to perturbation requires qualification. The finray is more robust than the
rigid jaw in the depth dimension (12$\times$ wider working range) but
\emph{less} robust than the rigid jaw in the tilt dimension at boundary
depths, where its asymmetric geometry introduces direction-dependent biases
that the symmetric rigid jaw does not exhibit. Robust cloth-handling system
design must therefore explicitly choose between:
\begin{enumerate}
  \item Operating the finray in the safe plateau ($+5$ to $+10$\,mm),
        accepting the higher contact force in exchange for full
        tilt-invariance, or
  \item Operating the finray near the boundary for delicate fabrics,
        accepting the need to model and control gripper tilt to
        sub-degree precision.
\end{enumerate}

The first choice corresponds to the open-loop ``set and grab'' deployment
appropriate for high-throughput textile processing; the second corresponds
to vision-or-tactile-guided closed-loop manipulation for delicate apparel.

\section{Limitations}

\TODO{Discussion of limitations:
\begin{itemize}
  \item Single cloth specimen, single surface; the working ranges
        will shift with cloth thickness, friction, and surface compliance.
  \item Only \texttt{finray\_26} received the full tilt sweep; other variants
        had only the $0^{\circ}$ depth baseline. Cross-variant tilt
        comparison is a clear next step.
  \item No tilt data on the rigid parallel jaws: the soft-vs-rigid tilt
        comparison must be inferred rather than measured.
  \item \texttt{finray\_18} and \texttt{finray\_26\_5deg} were not
        subjected to the y-axis asymmetry test; whether the handedness
        finding generalises across thicknesses is unknown.
  \item Sample size at the $-0.5$\,mm $0^{\circ}$ boundary cell was only
        $n=1$ in experiment 1, so the headline $58\%$ reduction claim
        rests on an underpowered baseline.
\end{itemize}}

